  <!--
    Cycles comes from big_libs, always. When RHINOCYCLESDEV is set,
    ccycles.vcxproj rebuilds it and installs it into big_libs first, so the copy
    below is the single route into the output either way - what someone who
    rebuilt Cycles runs is exactly what everyone else gets from the prebuilt
    payload. This used to be skipped when RHINOCYCLESDEV was set, which left the
    two cases taking different paths.

    The folder follows the configuration, but only release is committed to
    big_libs. The debug payload is 444 MB of binaries nobody but a Cycles
    developer has any use for, and a Debug build of Rhino ran against release
    Cycles for years without trouble. So the debug folder is used when it is
    actually there - which means someone ticked "Cycles Core" and built Debug
    locally - and release otherwise. A checkout with no local Cycles build gets a
    working ccycles.dll in every configuration.

    ReleaseDebuggable maps to release: build_cycles.ps1 builds it as
    RelWithDebInfo and installs it there.

    And "local" wins over both, in every configuration, but only while it is
    newer than the committed payload. That is the payload a narrow local build
    writes - build_cycles.ps1 builds kernels only for the GPUs in the machine,
    which is what both +Cycles configurations do - and it exists precisely
    because that build must not overwrite the committed payload with kernels for
    one card. It is gitignored in big_libs.

    The freshness test is what stops it becoming a trap. Preferring it merely
    because it exists would mean a developer who built +Cycles once kept
    deploying that build for ever, including after pulling commits that
    republished the payload - silently, months later. Comparing write times
    expires it by itself: git touches the committed ccycles.dll when a pull
    changes it, so the newer payload simply wins, and rebuilding +Cycles makes
    the local one win again. Nothing to remember and no folder to delete.

    The debug payload keeps its old behaviour - preferred whenever it exists, in
    Debug only - on purpose. Someone who built it wants to step into ccycles, and
    silently switching them to release Cycles because a pull was newer would take
    that away.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Derived from this project's own directory, not $(SolutionDir). The payload does
      not move when you build a different solution, but the old path did: building
      RhinoCycles.sln rather than Rhino.sln resolved it to
      src4\rhino4\Plug-ins\big_libs, which does not exist, and the post-build XCopy
      failed with "File not found - *" and took the build down with it. Nothing about
      where big_libs lives depends on which solution is open.

      RhinoCycles -> RDK -> Plug-ins -> rhino4 -> src4 -> repo root.
    -->
    <CyclesPayloadRoot>$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\..\..\..\..\..\big_libs\RhinoCycles\ccycles\win</CyclesPayloadRoot>
    <CyclesPayloadPath>$(CyclesPayloadRoot)\release</CyclesPayloadPath>
    <CyclesPayloadPath Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug' And Exists('$(CyclesPayloadRoot)\debug\ccycles.dll')">$(CyclesPayloadRoot)\debug</CyclesPayloadPath>

    <!--
      File.GetLastWriteTime returns 1601-01-01 for a path that does not exist
      rather than failing, so the release side needs no Exists guard - a missing
      committed payload just makes any local one newer, which is the right answer.
    -->
    <CyclesLocalTicks Condition="Exists('$(CyclesPayloadRoot)\local\ccycles.dll')">$([System.IO.File]::GetLastWriteTime('$(CyclesPayloadRoot)\local\ccycles.dll').Ticks)</CyclesLocalTicks>
    <CyclesReleaseTicks>$([System.IO.File]::GetLastWriteTime('$(CyclesPayloadRoot)\release\ccycles.dll').Ticks)</CyclesReleaseTicks>
    <CyclesPayloadPath Condition="'$(CyclesLocalTicks)' != '' And $(CyclesLocalTicks) &gt; $(CyclesReleaseTicks)">$(CyclesPayloadRoot)\local</CyclesPayloadPath>
    <!--
      /D on all three: copy only what is newer than the destination.

      RunPostBuildEvent is Always, so this runs on every build of this project -
      including one where nothing changed and the compile was skipped. Without
      /D that recopied the whole payload every time, which is 321 MB, 29 MB of
      it kernels. XCopy returns 0 when everything is already up to date, so a
      no-op copy does not fail the build.

      XCopy rather than Copy for the first line so it can take /D. No /S there,
      which keeps it non-recursive exactly as Copy was - lib and source are
      handled by the two lines below, into different destinations.
    -->
    <PostBuildEvent Condition="'$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT'">
      XCopy $(CyclesPayloadPath)\* $(OutputPath). /Y /D /I /Q
      XCopy $(CyclesPayloadPath)\lib $(OutputPath)RhinoCycles\lib /Y /D /I /S /Q
      XCopy $(CyclesPayloadPath)\source $(OutputPath)RhinoCycles\source /Y /D /I /S /Q
    </PostBuildEvent>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Say which payload was deployed. Which one you got was invisible before, and
    it is the difference between being able to step into ccycles and not - a
    Debug build silently falls back to the release payload whenever the debug one
    has not been built locally, because only release is committed to big_libs.

    A Message and not a Warning: the solution builds with zero warnings and that
    is worth keeping, and using release Cycles under a Debug Rhino is the normal
    case rather than a mistake.

    The fallback line is gated on RHINOCYCLESDEV. No fresh checkout has a debug
    payload - only release is committed - so an ungated message would tell every
    developer on every Debug build that native Cycles debugging is unavailable,
    which is only news to someone who wanted it. The flag being set is the
    closest thing to a statement of that intent.
  -->
  <Target Name="ReportCyclesPayload" AfterTargets="Build" Condition="'$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT'">
    <Message Importance="high" Text="RhinoCycles: deployed Cycles payload from $(CyclesPayloadPath)" />

    <!--
      A local payload that lost the freshness test is worth a line. Without one,
      a developer who built +Cycles some time ago and has since pulled sees their
      build stop being used with no explanation - which is the confusion the test
      was meant to prevent, moved one step along. Rebuilding +Cycles is the fix,
      and saying so beats leaving them to work it out.
    -->
    <Message Importance="high"
             Condition="'$(CyclesLocalTicks)' != '' And $(CyclesLocalTicks) &lt;= $(CyclesReleaseTicks)"
             Text="RhinoCycles: your local Cycles payload is older than the committed one, so the committed one was used. Rebuild a +Cycles configuration to use yours again." />
    <Message Importance="high"
             Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug' And '$(RHINOCYCLESDEV)' != '' And !Exists('$(CyclesPayloadRoot)\debug\ccycles.dll')"
             Text="RhinoCycles: no debug Cycles payload in big_libs, so this Debug build uses release Cycles. Native debugging into ccycles will not work. Build the solution once as Debug with RHINOCYCLESDEV set to produce one." />
  </Target>
